TASK: Extract the chronological chain of domestic legal proceedings from the provided European Court of Human Rights (ECHR) text.
Ensure that you model only domestic proceedings and events inc. related administrative procedures related to the case. DO NOT model legal precedent or other legal cases which did not DIRECTLY affect the applicants in the case under consideration.
The ultimate goal is to be able to construct a timeline of key events and legal procedures which led up to the legal decision or judgment rendered by the ECtHR

STRICT SCHEMA CONSTRAINTS:

    You must strictly adhere to the provided OWL/Turtle ontology.

    CRITICAL: Do not hallucinate or use any classes, properties, or vocabulary members that are not explicitly defined in the provided schema.

EXTRACTION RULES:

    Focus on Domestic Events: Extract only events and proceedings that occurred prior to the submission to the ECHR. Instantiate each as the most specific echr:DomesticEvent subclass it actually is: echr:DomesticProceeding for a step before a court or tribunal, echr:AdministrativeAction for a non-judicial decision by a state or municipal body (e.g. appointing a guardian, denying a welfare request), echr:EnforcementAction for the physical or bureaucratic execution of an earlier decision, and echr:ProsecutorialReview for an internal decision or appeal that stays inside a prosecutor's office without reaching a court. Do not force everything into echr:DomesticProceeding just because it is the most familiar class.

    Map the Procedural Chain: ECHR facts often feature multiple ongoing, parallel legal processes (e.g., simultaneous criminal and civil tracks). You must make the chronological and appellate connections between these proceedings explicit by linking them using the echr:followsProceeding property.

    Anchor All Evidence: Every extracted entity must be grounded in the source text. You must populate echr:hasSupportingQuote with an exact, verbatim substring extracted directly from the text. Do not paraphrase, truncate, or alter the quote in any way.

    Ensure distinct real-world entities are assigned strictly unique IRIs. Do not merge distinct courts, persons, or proceedings into a single node. An entity should generally have only one rdfs:label string.

    A single echr:DomesticProceeding represents exactly ONE jurisdictional instance (i.e. trial or appeal)
        - It is strictly forbidden for a proceeding to have more than one echr:hasCourt or more than one echr:hasDecisionDate.
        - If the text mentions a case moving to a new court, being appealed, or receiving a new decision on a different date, you MUST mint a completely NEW echr:DomesticProceeding IRI.
        - You must link the new proceeding to the old one using echr:followsProceeding. Never merge them.
        - This applies even when the earlier proceeding already exists as a node you were given (e.g. from earlier in this same document): finding a SECOND court or decision-maker for a matter that already has one is itself the signal to mint a new echr:DomesticProceeding, not to add a second echr:hasCourt to the node you already have. Never edit an existing proceeding to give it a second court, decision-maker, or decision date.

    Model the Parties: Create one echr:Party per person, company or public body taking part in a domestic event, and type a human party ALSO as echr:NaturalPerson (a company or public body is echr:Party alone). Link the future Strasbourg applicant(s) from the case document with echr:hasApplicant.
        - This applies even to a node you already minted as something else, such as echr:DomesticAuthority. The same real-world body can be the deciding authority in one event and a party in another (e.g. a municipal council that rules on a matter, then later appeals as a party). When that happens, ADD echr:Party as an additional rdf:type on the SAME node rather than creating a duplicate -- do not leave it typed only echr:DomesticAuthority just because that is how it first appeared, and do not skip minting the participation because the node "already exists". A node with no echr:Party type can never be a valid echr:participatingParty.
        - Record each party's involvement at each instance as a separate echr:Participation. Every echr:Participation you mint MUST carry all three of these links, or it is useless and you should not mint it at all: echr:hasParticipation pointing to it from the domestic event, exactly ONE echr:participatingParty pointing back to a node you typed echr:Party, and echr:hasPartySide for which side they were on (initiating, responding, third party, or unknown). A participation with no echr:participatingParty is a defect: never emit one.
        - echr:participatingParty must name the party ITSELF, never their lawyer, guardian or representative. A representative is not a participant in their own right - they are attached to the party they act for with echr:isRepresentedBy, and the participation still names the party. Do not create a participation for a lawyer.
        - A party who is the responding party at trial and the initiating party on appeal gets TWO participations, not one with two sides.
        - Where the text names a lawyer, legal-aid counsel or guardian acting for a party, create an echr:LegalRepresentative (a subclass of echr:NaturalPerson) and link it with echr:isRepresentedBy from the party.
